# ==============================================================================
# .gitattributes - deterministic line endings across platforms
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS
#
# Git's default behaviour depends on each contributor's local `core.autocrlf`
# setting. On this developer's machine it is `true`, which means Git rewrites LF
# to CRLF in the working copy on checkout. The repository stores LF either way,
# so the committed content is portable - but the FILES ON DISK are not, and some
# of them are executables by another interpreter.
#
# The concrete failure this prevents: a Makefile checked out with CRLF endings
# has every recipe line terminated with a carriage return. `make` hands that line
# to the shell verbatim, so the shell looks for a command called `pytest\r`,
# reports "command not found", and the target fails in a way that has nothing to
# do with the code. The same applies to any shell script in the repository.
#
# That failure is worst exactly where it hurts most: it appears on a new
# contributor's machine, on Windows, at the first `make test` - which is the first
# command the README tells them to run.
#
# `.gitattributes` fixes it in the repository rather than in each person's global
# Git configuration, which is the only approach that actually reaches everyone.
#
# WHAT IT DOES NOT DO
#
# It does not normalise the files already committed; they are already stored with
# LF in Git, which was verified. This file only governs future checkouts.
# ==============================================================================

# Normalise line endings to LF in the repository, and check them out as LF,
# regardless of any local core.autocrlf setting.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Files whose interpreter is line-ending-sensitive. Called out explicitly so the
# reason survives: these break at runtime, not at commit time, which is the kind
# of bug that gets misdiagnosed as something else entirely.
Makefile        text eol=lf
*.sh            text eol=lf
*.yml           text eol=lf
*.yaml          text eol=lf

# Windows-native development does exist in this project (the developer is on
# Windows), but nothing here compiles a Windows binary or ships a Windows
# launcher, so there is nothing to exempt. If a .bat or .cmd file is ever added,
# give it `text eol=crlf` in this file rather than relying on local settings.

# Binary assets must never be line-ending translated. Declared up front so a
# future image or fixture is never silently corrupted by a contributor's config.
*.png           binary
*.jpg           binary
*.jpeg          binary
*.gif           binary
*.ico           binary
*.pdf           binary
*.zip           binary
*.gz            binary
*.whl           binary